{
  "plan": {
    "operation": "change_assumption",
    "parent_shortcut": "fixed_environment",
    "response_change": {
      "type": "curvature_change",
      "parent_relation": "The daughter loss term is linear in N_D with an effective removal rate driven only by current daughter abundance and saturating against a fixed capacity, so for fixed N_P, N_D, and daughter_decay_constant history the target depends on N_D through a single first-order sink.",
      "child_relation": "Release the damage-capacity assumption by allowing the capacity of the daughter-loss environment itself to evolve under irradiation history, so the same instantaneous N_D can produce different future target curvature depending on accumulated environmental state.",
      "affected_inputs": [
        "N_D",
        "daughter_decay_constant",
        "damage_capacity"
      ],
      "observable_effect": "Across runs with the same parent decay constant and similar early N_D buildup, the daughter peak height and post-peak steepening separate: some trajectories show prolonged moderate loss before late acceleration, while others saturate the loss environment earlier and deplete sooner."
    },
    "diagnostic_groups": [
      {
        "name": "production_to_loss",
        "expression": "parent_decay_constant*N_P/(daughter_decay_constant*N_D + 1.0e-12)",
        "role": "instantaneous balance between daughter production and removal"
      },
      {
        "name": "capacity_loading",
        "expression": "daughter_decay_constant/(damage_capacity + 1.0e-12)",
        "role": "fractional approach to the current loss-environment saturation scale"
      },
      {
        "name": "daughter_burden",
        "expression": "N_D/(N_P + N_D + 1.0e-12)",
        "role": "tracked-population share carried by the daughter, indicating how strongly daughter-driven environmental evolution can matter"
      }
    ],
    "activation_condition": "Observable when daughter-driven environmental modification is neither negligible nor instantaneous: damage_drive_rate*N_D comparable to damage_relaxation_rate*damage_capacity over part of the sampled trajectory, so saturation effects occur after visible daughter buildup rather than being absent or immediate.",
    "train_hidden_contrast": "In training, the mechanism appears as gradual variation in peak sharpness and late-time curvature among trajectories with comparable early rise; in holdout, stronger or longer irradiation histories extend this into delayed versus early saturation of daughter loss that the fixed-capacity parent cannot match with one shared parameter set.",
    "continuation_path": "Recover the parent law when the capacity-evolution effect is turned off so the capacity remains constant over the experiment, or in the limit where its evolution is much slower than the observation window.",
    "freedom_change": "parameter_to_state",
    "promoted_symbol": "damage_capacity",
    "promotion_reason": "A fixed damage_capacity enforces one shared saturation scale for the daughter-loss environment. To represent irradiation-history-dependent carrying capacity of that environment, the capacity itself must evolve as a state; existing states cannot encode this without changing the target mapping only trivially.",
    "gap_statement": "The current model assumes the saturation scale of the daughter-loss environment is fixed, but in irradiated media the available loss pathways or trapping/escape structure can themselves grow or anneal during the run.",
    "baseline_assumption": "The finite saturation scale damage_capacity is time independent and unaffected by irradiation history.",
    "scientific_mechanism": "Radiation-induced restructuring changes the number of effective daughter-removal sites or channels, so the environment controlling daughter loss has an evolving capacity rather than a fixed one.",
    "affected_symbols": [
      "N_D",
      "daughter_decay_constant",
      "damage_capacity"
    ],
    "rationale": "A realistic next refinement is to relax the fixed-capacity assumption rather than adding an unrelated source or sink. The current target mapping already includes history dependence through daughter_decay_constant, but all trajectories are forced toward the same maximum effective loss scale set by one constant damage_capacity. Varying the irradiation history through the observed N_D trajectory while holding parent_decay_constant, initial N_P, and initial daughter_decay_constant fixed can therefore only change how quickly that fixed ceiling is approached, not the ceiling itself. If damage_capacity is promoted to a state, then accumulated daughter presence can alter the eventual saturation scale of daughter loss. This changes the scored mapping consequentially: dN_D_dt at later times no longer depends only on N_P, N_D, and the current daughter_decay_constant under one fixed ceiling, but on whether the environment has expanded or relaxed its capacity during the trajectory. The observable contrast is a changed daughter peak and post-peak curvature reachable under the stated initial conditions when daughter-driven environmental evolution is moderate. A single refitted parent parameter set cannot reproduce both delayed saturation with prolonged daughter residence and earlier stronger depletion across such conditions, because it has only one fixed saturation scale for all runs. Limitation: if capacity evolution is extremely slow or negligible on the observation window, the refinement collapses back to the parent and may not be distinguishable."
  },
